81: UnbiasedestimateofthetraceofHessian. Sincetheoutputdimension c andtheumberofparameters are all very large, computing the Jacobian ∇ θ f θ ( x − t ) will be very inefficient. We approximate the Jacobian with random samples as follows. Suppose f ( x − t ) is the predicted logits of the conditional probability, the trace of Hessian can be expressed as ∇ θ f θ ( x − t )] ˆ y ∥ 22 ] . To approximate this expectation, we can sample i.i.d. samples ˆ y from softmax ( f ( x − t )) and use the average as the unbiased estimate. For all experiments, we use number of samples 50. Details in Figure 3. To verify Theorem 3.3 that SGD biases the model towards flatter minima, we conduct MLM on PCFG and HMM-generated datasets with SGD. We set the proportion of warmup stage to 12 % total number of steps, and fix the learning rate to 1e-3 after the warmup. We evaluate the downstream performance and the trace of Hessian of different checkpoints along pre-training. The standard deviation of trace of Hessian is calculated based on 5 times of sampling 50 examples as mentioned above. Apart from the PCFG task C and HMM task-10, we also provide results on PCFG tasks A, B and HMM task-6 in Figure 10. n s t r e a A u r a c y % P r t r a i n i n g L o ss Viewing a small model as a special case of large model. We show that a small neural network is a special case of a large neural network by considering two cases (1) adding additional layers without changing the functionality and (2) adding more channels/attention heads without change the functionality. For (1), since the transformer contains residual connections, we can add new layers on top of a smaller model and fill in zeros to the added parameters. In this way, we can obtain a larger model with the same functionality as the small model. Concretely, suppose a small model is g θ , an additional block is h ϕ , and the large model can be expressed as f θ,ϕ ( · )= h ϕ ( g θ ( · ))+ g θ ( · ) . Then setting the parameters ϕ to all 0 can make f θ,ϕ ( · )= g θ ( · ) . For (2), since the transformers contain layer norm, we can duplicate the parameters channelwise to duplicate the feature dimension. Since we keep the mean and variance across channels unchanged, 16 the functionality will also be preserved. Similarly, we can also add more heads with this duplication technique. Therefore, the small models can be enlarged into a larger models with functionality preserved. The smaller transformer architecture can be viewed as a subset of the larger transformer architecture. In this sense, a set of transformers with different sizes and the same pre-training loss found in Section 2 can be viewed as a set of transformers with the same size. Note that the training algorithm only finds out the natural larger models, instead of the larger models which are augmented from the smaller models. This indicates that the implicit bias of the optimizer can interact with the model architecture. The implicit bias drives the model toward flat minima on both larger models and smaller models. The smaller transformer architecture is a subset of the larger transformer architecture, thus the flattest minima found with a larger transformer is flatter than the minima found with a smaller transformer. (See Figure 6). 17
