93: Theorem D.6. Suppose m ≥ ˜ O (2 T T 3 ϵ − 2 ) , and V i ∼N (0 ,TI 2 T ) for all i ∈ [ m ] . With probability at least 1 − δ over V , there exists ψ ′ ,u ′ , satisfying L ( ψ ′ ,u ′ ) ≤ ϵ and ∥ u ′ ∥ 22 ≤ O ( T 2 δ − 1 ) . Proof of Theorem D.6. Since the number of possible input in pre-training is finite, we can invoke Lemma 9 in Bai & Lee (2020) to show that random Gaussian features can fit the pre-training task. Lemma D.7 (Lemma 9 in Bai & Lee (2020)) . Suppose ∥ h ∥ 2 = (cid:113) 1 T , v ∼N (0 ,TI 2 T ) . There exists a random variable a ( v ) such that and a satisfies E v [ a 2 ]= O ( T 2 ) . Consider Q = K = 0 . In this case we have h Q,K ( x ) = 1 T (cid:80) Tj =1 x j . Also note that for the pre- training task, y = −√ T 1 ⊤ h Q,K ( x ) for all x . Since x j are norm 1 orthogonal to each other, we have ∥ h Q,K ( x ) ∥ 2 = 1 T for all x . Now we can show that V i ∼ N (0 ,TI 2 T ) , i ∈ [ m ] independently is the random feature solution which can solve the pre-training task. Suppose g ( h ) = 1 m (cid:80) mr =1 σ ( V ⊤ r h ) a ( V r ) , and g ( R ) ( h ) := 1 m (cid:80) mr =1 σ ( V ⊤ r h ) a ( V r ) I ( ∥ V r ∥ 2 ≤ √ TR ) . R is large enough such that Pr (cid:16) sup r ∈ [ m ] ∥ V r ∥ 2 ≥√ TR (cid:17) ≥ 1 − δ/ 2 . We have g ( h ) = g ( R ) ( h ) on this event. Let g ∗ ( R ) ( h ) be the truncated version of g ∗ ( h ) = −√ T 1 ⊤ h , g ∗ ( h ) ( R ) = E v [ σ ( v ⊤ h ) a ( v ) I ( ∥ v ∥ 2 ≤√ TR )] . We have By Chebyshev and a union bound, we have Pr For t = ϵ 2 , we have m ≥ n ( h ) R 2 T 2 ϵ − 2 . Choosing R = ˜ O ( (cid:112) ( T )) will make Pr (cid:16) ∥ v ∥ > √ TR (cid:17) 14 ≤ cϵT . Also note that n ( h )=( T/ 2 − 1) (cid:0) T T/ 2+1 (cid:1) . Thus m ≥ ˜ O (2 T T 3 ϵ − 2 ) suffices. 23
